WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.487
An expression such as this

00:00:02.571 --> 00:00:04.589
one is made of operands

00:00:04.589 --> 00:00:07.953
here the letters a to e, and operators

00:00:07.953 --> 00:00:11.654
here the four binary operators plus, minus

00:00:11.654 --> 00:00:13.168
times and divide.

00:00:13.168 --> 00:00:16.700
To know what it means, we need two rules.

00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:17.983
Precedence

00:00:18.068 --> 00:00:20.954
times and divide bind more tightly

00:00:21.039 --> 00:00:22.736
than plus and minus.

00:00:22.736 --> 00:00:24.009
Associativity

00:00:24.009 --> 00:00:26.639
all four are left-associative

00:00:26.639 --> 00:00:30.034
so among operators of equal precedence

00:00:30.034 --> 00:00:32.410
the leftmost is done first.

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:35.454
So 8 - 3 - 2

00:00:35.544 --> 00:00:39.495
means (8 - 3) - 2

00:00:39.495 --> 00:00:42.458
which is 3; not 8 -

00:00:42.458 --> 00:00:45.960
(3 - 2), which would be 7.

00:00:46.310 --> 00:00:49.667
With both rules, our expression means this

00:00:49.667 --> 00:00:51.306
fully parenthesised.

00:00:51.306 --> 00:00:54.820
Parentheses override precedence: in a *

00:00:54.820 --> 00:00:57.630
(b + c), the addition comes first.

00:00:58.210 --> 00:00:59.961
The usual way of writing

00:01:00.031 --> 00:01:02.343
with each binary operator between

00:01:02.413 --> 00:01:04.795
its two operands, is called infix.

00:01:04.865 --> 00:01:07.317
It needs parentheses and precedence

00:01:07.387 --> 00:01:09.559
rules before you can tell which

00:01:09.629 --> 00:01:11.170
operation comes first.

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:13.086
In postfix notation

00:01:13.086 --> 00:01:15.982
also called reverse Polish notation

00:01:15.982 --> 00:01:19.426
each operator comes after its two operands

00:01:19.426 --> 00:01:22.713
2 + 3 is written 2 3 +.

00:01:22.792 --> 00:01:24.983
Postfix needs no parentheses

00:01:25.062 --> 00:01:26.940
and no precedence rules

00:01:27.018 --> 00:01:29.680
the order of the operators already

00:01:29.758 --> 00:01:31.480
says what to do first.

00:01:31.830 --> 00:01:35.085
2 + 3 * 4 becomes

00:01:35.184 --> 00:01:37.748
2 3 4 * +.

00:01:37.748 --> 00:01:40.905
(2 + 3)

00:01:41.003 --> 00:01:43.765
* 4 becomes

00:01:43.864 --> 00:01:45.442
2 3 + 4 *.

00:01:45.442 --> 00:01:49.190
And our long expression becomes this.

00:01:49.540 --> 00:01:51.256
Notice that the operands

00:01:51.327 --> 00:01:52.972
stay in the same order

00:01:52.972 --> 00:01:54.759
only the operators move.

00:01:54.831 --> 00:01:57.118
And in postfix the operands must

00:01:57.190 --> 00:01:58.834
be separated by spaces

00:01:58.834 --> 00:02:01.265
2 3 4 is three operands

00:02:01.336 --> 00:02:03.696
while 234

00:02:03.767 --> 00:02:05.340
would be a single one.

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:08.677
How do we find the postfix form by hand?

00:02:08.677 --> 00:02:11.434
Find the operator that is applied last,

00:02:11.503 --> 00:02:13.570
the lowest-precedence operator,

00:02:13.639 --> 00:02:16.534
outside all parentheses, and among equals,

00:02:16.534 --> 00:02:17.843
the rightmost one.

00:02:17.912 --> 00:02:19.980
Its two operands are the parts

00:02:20.049 --> 00:02:22.048
on its left and on its right.

00:02:22.117 --> 00:02:24.046
Then the postfix form is the

00:02:24.115 --> 00:02:25.838
postfix of the left part

00:02:25.838 --> 00:02:27.975
the postfix of the right part

00:02:27.975 --> 00:02:29.560
and then the operator.

00:02:29.910 --> 00:02:31.866
Take 7 /

00:02:31.866 --> 00:02:35.688
(2 + 3) * 4.

00:02:35.688 --> 00:02:39.243
Divide and times have equal precedence

00:02:39.243 --> 00:02:42.977
so the rightmost, times, is applied last.

00:02:43.066 --> 00:02:45.732
Its operands are 7 /

00:02:45.821 --> 00:02:48.310
(2 + 3), and 4.

00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:50.133
In the left part

00:02:50.133 --> 00:02:52.991
the last operator is the divide

00:02:52.991 --> 00:02:56.456
with operands 7 and (2 + 3).

00:02:56.456 --> 00:03:00.180
And (2 + 3) becomes 2 3 +.

00:03:00.530 --> 00:03:03.932
Putting the pieces back together,

00:03:03.932 --> 00:03:08.469
reading the tree from the bottom up:

00:03:08.469 --> 00:03:12.490
7 2 3 + / 4 *.

00:03:12.840 --> 00:03:15.832
The same method turns 2 *

00:03:15.832 --> 00:03:18.356
(3 + 2 * 4)

00:03:18.356 --> 00:03:22.283
into 2 3 2 4 * + *,

00:03:22.283 --> 00:03:26.303
here the last operator is the first times

00:03:26.303 --> 00:03:28.640
outside the parentheses.

00:03:29.220 --> 00:03:31.523
The same recursion in Java works

00:03:31.595 --> 00:03:33.179
on an expression tree

00:03:33.251 --> 00:03:35.770
where each operator is a node whose

00:03:35.842 --> 00:03:38.001
two children are its operands;

00:03:38.001 --> 00:03:40.304
trees come in Lecture 13.

00:03:40.304 --> 00:03:42.103
A leaf holds an operand

00:03:42.103 --> 00:03:43.831
and is its own postfix.

00:03:43.903 --> 00:03:46.278
An inner node returns the postfix

00:03:46.350 --> 00:03:49.445
of its left child, then of its right child

00:03:49.445 --> 00:03:51.100
then its own operator.

00:03:51.450 --> 00:03:53.900
A parse method builds the tree from

00:03:53.970 --> 00:03:56.420
an infix string with the precedence

00:03:56.490 --> 00:03:58.170
and associativity rules

00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:00.480
and the demo's output reproduces

00:04:00.550 --> 00:04:02.090
the table we just saw.

00:04:02.670 --> 00:04:03.820
Now the payoff.

00:04:03.896 --> 00:04:05.812
Read a postfix expression

00:04:05.888 --> 00:04:07.344
from left to right,

00:04:07.421 --> 00:04:10.793
each operator applies to the two most recent

00:04:10.869 --> 00:04:13.551
values that have not been used yet.

00:04:13.551 --> 00:04:16.310
A stack holds exactly those values.

00:04:16.660 --> 00:04:18.758
So make an empty stack.

00:04:18.758 --> 00:04:21.038
and scan the expression.

00:04:21.038 --> 00:04:22.954
An operand: push it.

00:04:22.954 --> 00:04:26.237
An operator: pop the right operand

00:04:26.237 --> 00:04:28.700
then pop the left operand

00:04:28.700 --> 00:04:32.440
apply the operator, and push the result.

00:04:32.440 --> 00:04:34.538
When the scan is over

00:04:34.538 --> 00:04:38.460
the result is the only value on the stack.

00:04:38.810 --> 00:04:41.634
The order of the two pops matters

00:04:41.720 --> 00:04:43.517
for minus and divide.

00:04:43.517 --> 00:04:45.400
Take 8 2 -.

00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:47.369
We push 8 and 2.

00:04:47.369 --> 00:04:51.134
The first pop gives 2, the right operand

00:04:51.134 --> 00:04:54.387
the second gives 8, the left one

00:04:54.387 --> 00:04:57.810
and the result is 8 - 2, 6.

00:04:58.390 --> 00:05:02.544
Let's evaluate 6 2 / 3

00:05:02.663 --> 00:05:05.749
- 4 2 * +

00:05:05.868 --> 00:05:09.547
the postfix form of 6 /

00:05:09.666 --> 00:05:14.413
(2 - 3) + 4 * 2.

00:05:14.413 --> 00:05:16.787
Push 6, push 2.

00:05:16.787 --> 00:05:19.754
/ pops 2 and 6

00:05:19.754 --> 00:05:24.265
and pushes 6 / 2: 3.

00:05:24.265 --> 00:05:25.689
Push 3.

00:05:25.689 --> 00:05:31.030
- pops 3 and 3, and pushes 0.

00:05:31.380 --> 00:05:33.172
Push 4, push 2.

00:05:33.172 --> 00:05:37.026
* pops 2 and 4, and pushes 8.

00:05:37.026 --> 00:05:40.969
+ pops 8 and 0, and pushes 8.

00:05:40.969 --> 00:05:44.913
The scan is over, and the answer is on top:

00:05:44.913 --> 00:05:45.540
8.

00:05:46.120 --> 00:05:47.131
Your turn.

00:05:47.232 --> 00:05:49.960
Evaluate 2 3 2 4

00:05:50.061 --> 00:05:51.779
* + *,

00:05:51.779 --> 00:05:54.912
the postfix form of 2 *

00:05:54.912 --> 00:05:57.640
(3 + 2 * 4).

00:05:57.990 --> 00:05:59.334
What is the value

00:05:59.409 --> 00:06:02.395
and what is the largest number of values

00:06:02.470 --> 00:06:04.486
on the stack at any moment?

00:06:04.486 --> 00:06:06.950
Pause the video and work it out.

00:06:10.530 --> 00:06:14.369
Push 2, 3, 2 and 4: four values,

00:06:14.369 --> 00:06:16.511
the most at any moment.

00:06:16.511 --> 00:06:19.636
* gives 2 * 4, 8,

00:06:19.636 --> 00:06:22.760
so the stack is 2, 3, 8.

00:06:22.760 --> 00:06:26.064
+ gives 3 + 8, 11.

00:06:26.064 --> 00:06:29.099
And * gives 2 * 11,

00:06:29.099 --> 00:06:30.170
22.

00:06:30.750 --> 00:06:31.359
In Java,

00:06:31.435 --> 00:06:34.785
evaluate splits the expression at the spaces

00:06:34.861 --> 00:06:37.601
and uses our LinkedStack of Integer.

00:06:37.601 --> 00:06:38.896
For an operator,

00:06:38.896 --> 00:06:41.408
it pops the right operand first

00:06:41.408 --> 00:06:43.539
because it was pushed last.

00:06:43.539 --> 00:06:44.986
then the left one.

00:06:44.986 --> 00:06:46.889
If the left one is null

00:06:46.889 --> 00:06:48.640
an operand is missing

00:06:48.640 --> 00:06:50.771
and it throws an exception.

00:06:50.771 --> 00:06:53.892
Otherwise, it pushes the result of apply.

00:06:53.968 --> 00:06:55.719
An operand is parsed as

00:06:55.795 --> 00:06:57.470
an integer and pushed.

00:06:58.050 --> 00:07:00.609
After the scan, it pops the result

00:07:00.609 --> 00:07:03.094
and the stack must then be empty

00:07:03.167 --> 00:07:05.141
otherwise the input was not

00:07:05.214 --> 00:07:06.676
a single expression.

00:07:06.676 --> 00:07:08.358
It returns the result.

00:07:08.431 --> 00:07:10.478
The helper apply is a switch

00:07:10.551 --> 00:07:11.720
on the operator

00:07:11.720 --> 00:07:14.571
and because the operands are integers

00:07:14.571 --> 00:07:17.130
divide is Java's integer division.

00:07:17.710 --> 00:07:21.043
The demo prints 8 and 22

00:07:21.136 --> 00:07:22.895
for our two traces.

00:07:22.988 --> 00:07:25.488
7 2 3 + /

00:07:25.580 --> 00:07:29.469
4 * gives 4, not 5.6

00:07:29.562 --> 00:07:32.524
because 7 / 5 is

00:07:32.617 --> 00:07:35.024
1 in integer arithmetic.

00:07:35.024 --> 00:07:37.617
And 8 2 - is 6

00:07:37.617 --> 00:07:40.950
while 2 8 - is -6.

00:07:41.300 --> 00:07:44.559
The stack also catches malformed input.

00:07:44.559 --> 00:07:47.901
With too few operands, like 3 +

00:07:47.901 --> 00:07:50.742
an operator finds an empty stack

00:07:50.742 --> 00:07:52.914
missing operand for +.

00:07:52.914 --> 00:07:55.588
With too many, like 3 4

00:07:55.588 --> 00:07:58.011
more than one value is left

00:07:58.011 --> 00:08:00.100
not a single expression.

00:08:00.680 --> 00:08:02.078
One classic bug:

00:08:02.078 --> 00:08:05.661
popping the operands in the wrong order.

00:08:05.661 --> 00:08:08.370
int left = stack.pop()

00:08:08.370 --> 00:08:11.604
then int right = stack.pop()

00:08:11.604 --> 00:08:15.274
looks natural, but it swaps the operands

00:08:15.274 --> 00:08:19.119
8 2 - would give 2 - 8

00:08:19.119 --> 00:08:20.080
-6.

00:08:20.430 --> 00:08:23.586
Addition and multiplication hide the bug

00:08:23.586 --> 00:08:26.510
so always test with minus and divide.

00:08:27.090 --> 00:08:27.905
To sum up.

00:08:27.905 --> 00:08:30.514
1. 后缀表达式将每个运算符

00:08:30.596 --> 00:08:32.144
after its operands

00:08:32.226 --> 00:08:34.346
so it needs no parentheses

00:08:34.427 --> 00:08:36.384
and no precedence rules.

00:08:36.384 --> 00:08:38.666
2. 递归地推导它：

00:08:38.666 --> 00:08:42.253
with the operator applied last at the root

00:08:42.253 --> 00:08:44.699
the postfix of the left part

00:08:44.699 --> 00:08:47.226
the postfix of the right part

00:08:47.226 --> 00:08:48.775
then the operator.

00:08:48.775 --> 00:08:51.465
3: evaluate it with a stack.

00:08:51.465 --> 00:08:52.688
Push operands

00:08:52.688 --> 00:08:55.623
an operator pops the right operand

00:08:55.623 --> 00:08:58.720
then the left, and pushes the result.

00:08:58.720 --> 00:09:00.921
Each token costs one push

00:09:00.921 --> 00:09:03.530
or two pops and a push: O(n).

00:09:03.880 --> 00:09:05.942
That completes Lecture 9.

00:09:05.942 --> 00:09:08.593
A stack is touched only at its top

00:09:08.667 --> 00:09:10.728
it can live in a linked list

00:09:10.802 --> 00:09:12.422
or in a growing array

00:09:12.422 --> 00:09:14.484
with O(1) push and pop

00:09:14.557 --> 00:09:17.429
and in one left-to-right pass it checks

00:09:17.503 --> 00:09:20.080
brackets and evaluates expressions.

00:09:20.430 --> 00:09:24.574
Try this: evaluate 5 1 2 + 4

00:09:24.675 --> 00:09:27.000
* + 3 -

00:09:27.101 --> 00:09:29.526
and write down the stack

00:09:29.627 --> 00:09:31.649
after each operator.

00:09:31.649 --> 00:09:33.670
What is the result?

00:09:34.020 --> 00:09:36.544
Next, in Lecture 10: queues

00:09:36.544 --> 00:09:40.460
where the first element in is the first out.

